1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,704
Your deployment is green. Then you roll back,
and a new customer order

2
00:00:07,704 --> 00:00:15,407
disappears. I'm Shivam Gupta. UndoProof
rehearses the recovery plan before a database
migration

3
00:00:15,407 --> 00:00:16,000
ships.

4
00:00:16,000 --> 00:00:22,500
This is our checkout example. The release works,
but its rollback quietly loses

5
00:00:22,500 --> 00:00:24,000
a new order.

6
00:00:24,000 --> 00:00:30,000
A release contract makes the migration,
application queries, and important data
explicit.

7
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:36,500
Here is the problem. The rollback drops the
current orders table and restores

8
00:00:36,500 --> 00:00:39,000
a snapshot taken before deployment.

9
00:00:39,000 --> 00:00:45,118
This write creates order one hundred and four
after deployment. Our recovery plan

10
00:00:45,118 --> 00:00:47,000
must preserve that order.

11
00:00:47,000 --> 00:00:54,429
Let's rehearse. UndoProof runs real PostgreSQL
through PGlite. The result blocks this release

12
00:00:54,429 --> 00:00:55,000
plan.

13
00:00:55,000 --> 00:01:01,500
The forward path works. The rollback SQL also
succeeds. But data preservation fails.

14
00:01:01,500 --> 00:01:04,000
Those are deliberately separate checks.

15
00:01:04,000 --> 00:01:10,118
Here is the evidence. Four rows before rollback,
three afterward. The highlighted new

16
00:01:10,118 --> 00:01:16,235
order has disappeared. We compare exact values
in the supplied invariant projection, so

17
00:01:16,235 --> 00:01:20,000
a successful SQL command cannot hide this
failure.

18
00:01:20,000 --> 00:01:25,318
This export gives Bob the exact contract and
failure evidence. It is a

19
00:01:25,318 --> 00:01:29,000
manual IDE handoff, ready for a focused repair
task.

20
00:01:29,000 --> 00:01:34,828
For this example, we include a repair to review.
It keeps the compatible

21
00:01:34,828 --> 00:01:40,655
expanded schema when the application rolls back.
Removing the destructive snapshot restore
preserves

22
00:01:40,655 --> 00:01:42,000
the new data.

23
00:01:42,000 --> 00:01:50,125
Apply the reviewed SQL and run the same engine
again. This time every

24
00:01:50,125 --> 00:01:52,000
supplied check passes.

25
00:01:52,000 --> 00:01:58,000
All four orders survive, including the write
after deployment. The earlier application
queries

26
00:01:58,000 --> 00:02:04,000
still work. Rolling back application code does
not require deleting the expanded schema.

27
00:02:04,000 --> 00:02:10,118
The evidence packet includes executed SQL, data
comparisons, measured runtime, and a hash

28
00:02:10,118 --> 00:02:12,000
identifying the tested contract.

29
00:02:12,000 --> 00:02:17,909
History keeps both outcomes. Developers can
import their own contracts, and the command-line

30
00:02:17,909 --> 00:02:22,000
runner uses the same engine as a release gate.

31
00:02:22,000 --> 00:02:28,842
The Bob workflow starts with repository context
and this concrete failure. A developer

32
00:02:28,842 --> 00:02:35,684
asks Bob to inspect a repair, reviews the
proposed change, and rehearses again.

33
00:02:35,684 --> 00:02:42,000
The handoff never substitutes an AI confidence
score for executed database evidence.

34
00:02:42,000 --> 00:02:48,067
UndoProof is open source. Our proposed team
product adds shared policies and retained

35
00:02:48,067 --> 00:02:54,133
evidence. These results cover the tested SQL and
fixtures. The way back belongs

36
00:02:54,133 --> 00:02:56,000
in the release review.
